<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
        Only for an ad-hoc signed build, and only because of one rule.

        The hardened runtime turns on library validation, which requires every
        library loaded into the process to be signed by the same team as the
        application. Ad-hoc signing expresses no team at all, so an ad-hoc app
        and an ad-hoc framework are not "the same team" — they are two things
        with no team — and dyld refuses the framework with "different Team IDs".
        The application does not start.

        A Developer ID gives both halves the same team and this file stops being
        needed. Until there is one, this is the difference between a build that
        runs and a build that does not, and it is scoped to the ad-hoc case so
        that a signed build keeps the full hardened runtime.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
